<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Schleissheim Palace</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Schleissheim Palace is a group of three palaces at Oberschleissheim, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>. Beside an early 17th-century country residence of Duke William V and Elector Maximilian I, Elector <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-ii-emanuel-elector-of-bavaria">Max Emanuel</a> built the small Lustheim garden palace and, from 1701, the large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> New Palace, intended as a residence fit for an emperor. Its formal gardens are among the few Baroque gardens in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> to survive largely intact.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/schleissheim-palace.jpg" alt="Schleissheim Palace" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carsten Steger · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_image_of_the_Schleissheim_Palace.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Schleissheim Palace, at Oberschleißheim near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, is a complex of three palaces, the Old, Lustheim and New, set in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> park. It was a summer residence of the Bavarian rulers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">House of Wittelsbach</a>. The Old Palace began as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> country house and hermitage founded by William V in 1598 close to Dachau Palace. Under his son Maximilian I the buildings were extended between 1617 and 1623 by Heinrich Schön and Hans Krumpper, in the style of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrea-palladio">Andrea Palladio</a>, and the rooms were decorated by Peter Candid. Maximilian's son Ferdinand Maria died there in 1679.</p><p>Enrico Zuccalli built Lustheim as a garden villa in Italian style in 1684–1688 for Maximilian II Emanuel and his first wife, Maria Antonia. Zuccalli then erected the New Palace in 1701–1704 as the new residence, because the elector expected the imperial crown. Work stopped after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-ii-emanuel-elector-of-bavaria">Max Emanuel</a> lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> for some years in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>. Joseph Effner enlarged the building in 1719–1726, but only the main wing was completed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The New Palace is more than 300 metres long, and its main building has 37 window bays on the garden side. Its grand staircase by Zuccalli, which later inspired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balthasar-neumann">Balthasar Neumann</a>, rises in a high hall under a dome fresco by Cosmas Damian Asam. The Grand Gallery follows a draft by Robert de Cotte, and the interiors celebrate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-ii-emanuel-elector-of-bavaria">Max Emanuel</a>'s victories as a general against the Turks. His son Emperor Charles VII Albert preferred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nymphenburg-palace">Nymphenburg Palace</a>, so only one of four planned wings was completed. Maximilian III Joseph had some rooms redecorated in Rococo style, and under King Ludwig I Leo von Klenze completed the Great Staircase.</p><p>The park is one of the rare <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> gardens in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> to survive largely preserved. Zuccalli laid out its canals, and Dominique Girard, a pupil of Le Nôtre, built the grand parterre and the cascade by 1720.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The palaces were heavily destroyed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and later reconstructed. The Old Palace houses exhibitions on religious culture and on the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, Lustheim has held a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a> porcelain collection since 1968, and the New Palace shows the gallery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> paintings owned by the Bavarian State Picture Collections, including works by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-paul-rubens">Peter Paul Rubens</a> and Anthony van Dyck. The grounds include a beer garden with seating for 1,000, and the palace has served as a film location, notably for Paths of Glory in 1957.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Schleissheim Palace” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The electors built Nymphenburg and <b>Schleissheim</b> palaces in and near Munich.</blockquote><a href="/state/electorate-of-bavaria">Electorate of Bavaria</a></li></ul></section>
